WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:09.510
Mangroves are some of
the most diverse habitats

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:10.528
on the planet.

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:14.880
These coastal forests which
have adapted to live in

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:18.450
constantly changing saltwater
tides are home to sharks,

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:20.730
manatees and tigers.

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:23.821
But people depend on mangroves too.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.320
So far, the protection
offered to coastal areas

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.060
hasn't been enough of an incentive

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:53.253
to save this precious ecosystem.

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:18.000
In the decade up to 2020, the
area of mangroves decreased

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.253
by over one million hectares globally.

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:24.240
This is especially
troubling in West Africa,

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:27.843
where 80% of the population
live on the Atlantic Coast.

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:48.570
Rising sea levels have
caused widespread erosion

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:50.700
and flooding in low lying areas.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:52.500
While rising temperatures and drought

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:56.190
accelerate the evaporation of
water and increase salinity,

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:57.747
killing off mangroves.

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:23.280
Senegal has been hit particularly hard,

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:25.200
losing a third of its mangrove forest

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:28.620
between 1950 and the early 2000s.

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:30.720
In a village at the mouth
of the Senegal River,

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:34.050
1,500 people have
already been moved inland

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:36.150
to makeshift camps.

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:39.420
Authorities have warned
that at least 15,000 people

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:40.980
are at risk of being displaced

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:43.050
as rising sea levels flood homes,

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:44.973
mosques and agricultural land.

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:49.950
In a village called Djiffer,
inhabitants have already seen

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:52.140
most of their mangrove forest disappear

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:54.323
while the coast continues to erode.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:30.780
In an effort to avoid more flooding,

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:33.630
inhabitants of Djiffer are
taking drastic measures

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:36.000
such as rolling giant
boulders into the sea

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.823
to act as barriers against
the rising sea levels.

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:20.670
Some communities are
taking other action too

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:23.583
and replanting their
lost mangrove forests.

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:26.910
They could store up to 7 times more carbon

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:29.700
than terrestrial forests,
reducing climate change

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:33.750
and protect villages from
coastal erosion and flooding.

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:36.900
They could also offer residents
greater economic benefits

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:38.493
than they've ever had before.

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:56.618
Mangroves are food factories.

00:04:56.618 --> 00:04:58.890
The roots provide a surface that oysters

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:01.203
and other mollusks can
colonize and grow on.

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:04.620
And the complex structure
of meandering channels

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:07.443
offers a sanctuary to many
different kinds of fish.

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:28.080
For centuries, in villages
like Dassilame Serere,

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:29.790
people had relied on the mangroves

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:32.550
to harvest oysters and other seafood.

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:36.003
As the forest disappeared,
so too did this way of life.

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:40.920
But following a grant
from the United Nations,

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:43.689
the community have been able
to replant their forests

00:05:43.689 --> 00:05:46.499
and begin farming again.

00:06:20.807 --> 00:06:23.970
Slowly but surely, the
forests have grown back.

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:26.340
And the community also made other changes

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:29.463
to make sure both mangroves
and oysters would thrive.

00:06:45.390 --> 00:06:47.970
The workers put stakes
with thin ropes in the mud

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:50.070
for the oysters to latch onto.

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:52.590
This reduces pressure on
the remaining mangroves,

00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:54.273
and lets the oysters grow bigger.

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:59.400
Farmers can now sell fresh
oysters to local seaside resorts

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:01.260
or can them for a much higher profit

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:02.703
than they ever could before.

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:28.710
All around the young mangroves,

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:30.960
the villagers have seen
the return of fish,

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:34.260
crabs and other species
that had disappeared.

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:37.260
It's something that could be
replicated all over the world

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:38.880
as a tool to help communities

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:41.703
while global efforts are
made to fight climate change.

